# .shared-llm.yaml.example
#
# HOW IT WORKS
# ─────────────
# The planning flows (`/do-plan` on Pi, `/cc-plan` in Claude Code) write two
# files — plan.md and plan.html — into a directory you control. You then open
# the HTML file in a browser to read and annotate it.
#
# Two things must be configured together:
#
#   1. WHERE FILES ARE WRITTEN  →  "work_log.dir" in this file
#   2. WHERE YOUR HTTP SERVER SERVES FROM  →  the --directory arg of the server
#
# The "work_log.dir" path must be INSIDE the directory your server serves, so
# the HTML file has a URL you can actually open.
#
#
# STEP-BY-STEP SETUP
# ──────────────────
# 1. Pick a docs/plans root on your machine, e.g. ~/my-project/docs
#
# 2. Start a static HTTP server from that root:
#
#      python3 -m http.server 8089 --directory ~/my-project/docs
#
#    (run this in a background terminal or add it to a startup script)
#
# 3. Set "work_log.dir" below to a path INSIDE that root:
#
#      work_log:
#        dir: /home/<you>/my-project/docs/plans/{date}/{slug}
#
#    planning will write:
#      /home/<you>/my-project/docs/plans/2026-06-29/redesign-auth/plan.md
#      /home/<you>/my-project/docs/plans/2026-06-29/redesign-auth/plan.html
#
# 4. Open the HTML file in your browser at:
#
#      http://localhost:8089/plans/2026-06-29/redesign-auth/plan.html
#
#
# TOKENS
# ──────
#   {date}  →  YYYY-MM-DD  (today)
#   {slug}  →  your planning topic, lowercased and hyphenated
#   {type}  →  "plan"
#   {n}     →  next version integer, auto-incremented by scanning siblings
#
# A relative "work_log.dir" path resolves from the directory holding THIS file.
# An absolute path is used as-is.
#
#
# WHICH FILE WINS
# ───────────────
# Resolution walks UP from the current directory and takes the nearest
# `.shared-llm.yaml` that CONTAINS a `work_log:` mapping. A `.shared-llm.yaml`
# without that key is skipped and the walk continues — so `~/.shared-llm.yaml`,
# the machine-level roster of kit destinations, never shadows a repo's config.
# One file at your repo root therefore covers everything inside it.
#
# With nothing configured anywhere, plans land in
# /var/tmp/work-log/{date}/{slug}. Fine for throwaway use, inconvenient when
# you want plans versioned next to your work.
#
# A configured "work_log.dir" must be a non-empty string — a malformed value
# fails loudly rather than silently falling back to the default.
#
# For a single run, `$WORK_LOG_DIR` overrides this file, and `--dir <path>`
# passed through the planning command overrides both.
#
#
# REMOTE SESSIONS (optional "work_log.host")
# ──────────────────────────────────────────
# When you reach this machine by name instead of localhost — Tailscale, LAN,
# SSH from a laptop — set "work_log.host" to that machine name. Every URL the
# planning flows hand out (the planish pages on port 4390 AND the static
# grill/plan pages) then uses it instead of localhost, and the planish server
# binds 0.0.0.0 so those remote connections are accepted. Default: localhost.
# `$WORK_LOG_HOST` overrides it for a single session.
#
#   work_log:
#     host: your-machine-name
#
# "host" works on its own too — a `work_log:` block with only "host" keeps the
# default /var/tmp/work-log/{date}/{slug} directory.
#
#
# DEPRECATED PREDECESSORS
# ───────────────────────
# The old `.planish.yaml` (top-level `dir:` / `host:`), `$PLANISH_DIR`, and
# `$PLANISH_HOST` are still honored as a last-resort fallback and warn on
# stderr. Move them into this file's `work_log:` block — the fallback goes away
# in a future release.
#
#
# ALSO READ BY: the /design-doc skill (skills/design-doc/SKILL.md) reuses
# "work_log.host" for its own base_url (http://{host}:8089) when it has no more
# specific config of its own — one hostname for every doc/plan URL this kit
# hands out. See that skill's Step 0 for its own explicit-override option.
#
#
# COPY AND RENAME TO ACTIVATE
# ────────────────────────────
# cp .shared-llm.yaml.example .shared-llm.yaml
# Then edit the "dir" line below to match your setup.

work_log:
  dir: /absolute/path/to/your/docs/plans/{date}/{slug}
  # host: your-machine-name

# ── Alternative layouts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# If your server root IS your repo root (e.g. python3 -m http.server 8089):
# work_log:
#   dir: plans/{date}/{slug}

# If you use an ops/docs directory structure:
# work_log:
#   dir: ops/docs/work-log/{date}/{slug}/plan

# Versioned plans (v1, v2, …) in a named folder:
# work_log:
#   dir: plans/{slug}/v{n}
